WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.690
In this lesson you'll learn what your job becomes

2
00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.173
when AI writes, runs and fixes the code for you:

3
00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.088
judging the things you can't see,

4
00:00:09.088 --> 00:00:11.352
above all whether it's safe.

5
00:00:11.352 --> 00:00:13.883
You can only trust AI on what can actually

6
00:00:13.883 --> 00:00:16.949
be checked, by a machine, by you,

7
00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:18.888
or by someone who knows.

8
00:00:18.888 --> 00:00:20.757
Anything past that line,

9
00:00:20.757 --> 00:00:22.626
you either learn to check,

10
00:00:22.626 --> 00:00:24.855
or hand to someone who can.

11
00:00:24.855 --> 00:00:26.457
Code that runs and leaks

12
00:00:26.457 --> 00:00:28.839
is the new normal failure.

13
00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:31.936
This lesson sits at the Start of the map,

14
00:00:31.936 --> 00:00:34.328
in Orientation, because it changes

15
00:00:34.328 --> 00:00:36.952
how you walk every district after it.

16
00:00:36.952 --> 00:00:39.761
Over every district hang the lanterns.

17
00:00:39.761 --> 00:00:43.488
AI is the lantern: it lights every district,

18
00:00:43.488 --> 00:00:45.880
but it doesn't walk the streets for you.

19
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.749
This lesson is about the walking:

20
00:00:47.749 --> 00:00:49.757
the checks that stay yours.

21
00:00:49.757 --> 00:00:52.822
There are four, and each has a home later.

22
00:00:52.822 --> 00:00:54.761
Packages, in The Code.

23
00:00:54.761 --> 00:00:56.793
Review, in The History.

24
00:00:56.793 --> 00:00:58.697
Security, in Trust.

25
00:00:58.697 --> 00:01:01.868
And going live, in Shipping.

26
00:01:02.318 --> 00:01:04.663
Aaron, who made this course,

27
00:01:04.663 --> 00:01:09.400
once wrote Python in Notepad and saved it as .txt.

28
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.488
Today an agent would do all of that for him.

29
00:01:12.488 --> 00:01:16.273
Five years ago, AI in coding meant autocomplete.

30
00:01:16.273 --> 00:01:18.491
You ran the program, read the error,

31
00:01:18.491 --> 00:01:21.324
searched for it, and fixed it yourself.

32
00:01:21.324 --> 00:01:24.180
In 2022, you could paste the error into a chat,

33
00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.735
and paste the fix back.

34
00:01:25.735 --> 00:01:29.067
Since 2025, the agent runs the code,

35
00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:31.134
reads its own error, fixes it,

36
00:01:31.134 --> 00:01:33.944
and runs it again until the check passes.

37
00:01:33.944 --> 00:01:36.649
Aaron hasn't had to debug an error himself

38
00:01:36.649 --> 00:01:38.994
since he started using Claude Code.

39
00:01:38.994 --> 00:01:41.409
Writing and debugging code by hand

40
00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:43.824
is quickly becoming the exception.

41
00:01:43.824 --> 00:01:45.693
Google says about three quarters

42
00:01:45.693 --> 00:01:48.874
of its new code is now written by AI.

43
00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:51.777
Anthropic says most of its own is.

44
00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:54.227
Notice why crashes went first:

45
00:01:54.227 --> 00:01:56.920
a crash is something a machine can check.

46
00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:00.148
Andrej Karpathy calls the careful version

47
00:02:00.148 --> 00:02:02.272
agentic engineering:

48
00:02:02.272 --> 00:02:05.409
you direct, and you oversee.

49
00:02:05.858 --> 00:02:07.298
Here's the picture to carry through

50
00:02:07.298 --> 00:02:08.981
the rest of this lesson.

51
00:02:08.981 --> 00:02:11.489
AI is a tireless building crew.

52
00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:13.381
You are the owner who signs off.

53
00:02:13.381 --> 00:02:15.994
The crew builds a wall, checks that it stands,

54
00:02:15.994 --> 00:02:18.838
and rebuilds it if it falls. In the morning,

55
00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:20.545
you walk through the finished house.

56
00:02:20.545 --> 00:02:21.706
The lights come on.

57
00:02:21.706 --> 00:02:24.098
The taps run. It works.

58
00:02:24.098 --> 00:02:27.546
For years, that was the whole test: does it stand?

59
00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:29.926
The crew answers that one now.

60
00:02:29.926 --> 00:02:32.236
So the owner's question changes.

61
00:02:32.236 --> 00:02:34.955
What can't I see from in here?

62
00:02:35.405 --> 00:02:37.506
The errors that matter have moved.

63
00:02:37.506 --> 00:02:40.502
They used to be loud: the program crashed.

64
00:02:40.502 --> 00:02:43.625
Now they're quiet: the program works perfectly,

65
00:02:43.625 --> 00:02:46.017
and someone else can walk right in.

66
00:02:46.017 --> 00:02:48.002
Above all, that means security

67
00:02:48.002 --> 00:02:50.115
holes in code that runs.

68
00:02:50.115 --> 00:02:51.961
In Veracode's tests this summer,

69
00:02:51.961 --> 00:02:54.945
AI-written code was almost always valid.

70
00:02:54.945 --> 00:02:57.174
But only about half of it passed the security

71
00:02:57.174 --> 00:03:00.250
checks, about the same as a year earlier.

72
00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:03.351
Everything else got better; that number didn't,

73
00:03:03.351 --> 00:03:05.905
and bigger models barely moved it.

74
00:03:05.905 --> 00:03:09.342
An early study, from 2022, found people using

75
00:03:09.342 --> 00:03:12.244
an AI assistant wrote less secure code,

76
00:03:12.244 --> 00:03:14.833
and felt more sure it was secure.

77
00:03:14.833 --> 00:03:16.460
Why is it invisible?

78
00:03:16.923 --> 00:03:19.988
Security is about what someone else can do.

79
00:03:19.988 --> 00:03:21.962
You test the app as yourself.

80
00:03:21.962 --> 00:03:24.796
An attacker tests it as everyone else.

81
00:03:25.245 --> 00:03:28.333
In January, an app its founder said was built

82
00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:31.967
entirely by AI left its database open to anyone.

83
00:03:31.967 --> 00:03:34.707
The app's public key sat in the web page,

84
00:03:34.707 --> 00:03:35.880
which is normal.

85
00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.121
But a setting called row-level security

86
00:03:38.121 --> 00:03:41.662
was switched off, so that key opened everything.

87
00:03:41.662 --> 00:03:44.077
Anyone could read the data, and change it,

88
00:03:44.077 --> 00:03:46.851
including emails and API tokens.

89
00:03:46.851 --> 00:03:49.452
It was fixed within hours of the report.

90
00:03:49.452 --> 00:03:51.925
In May, researchers scanned hundreds

91
00:03:51.925 --> 00:03:54.978
of thousands of apps made with AI app builders,

92
00:03:54.978 --> 00:03:57.950
and found thousands exposing private data.

93
00:03:57.950 --> 00:03:59.425
Many platforms make a new

94
00:03:59.425 --> 00:04:01.305
project public by default,

95
00:04:01.305 --> 00:04:03.871
and the builders never knew to switch it.

96
00:04:03.871 --> 00:04:06.762
Words you'll meet: row-level security,

97
00:04:06.762 --> 00:04:09.991
public, anon key, exposed.

98
00:04:10.442 --> 00:04:12.950
You ask: make sure it's secure.

99
00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:15.887
And the agent answers: yes, it's secure.

100
00:04:15.887 --> 00:04:18.012
That yes is just another output,

101
00:04:18.012 --> 00:04:20.415
from the same crew that built the house.

102
00:04:20.415 --> 00:04:24.340
A true yes and a false yes look exactly the same.

103
00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:26.406
And self-reports can be wrong.

104
00:04:26.406 --> 00:04:30.470
In 2025, an agent on Replit deleted a company's

105
00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.117
production database during a code freeze,

106
00:04:33.117 --> 00:04:36.379
then said it couldn't be restored. It could.

107
00:04:36.379 --> 00:04:38.573
A study this summer caught agents passing

108
00:04:38.573 --> 00:04:41.626
every test while the real thing barely worked.

109
00:04:41.626 --> 00:04:43.438
This is the wall.

110
00:04:43.438 --> 00:04:45.748
Past it, there are two doors.

111
00:04:45.748 --> 00:04:47.826
Learn enough to check it yourself.

112
00:04:47.826 --> 00:04:50.961
Or bring in someone who can:

113
00:04:50.961 --> 00:04:52.888
an expert who reviews it.

114
00:04:52.888 --> 00:04:54.746
Even the companies selling AI

115
00:04:54.746 --> 00:04:57.427
security tools agree:

116
00:04:57.427 --> 00:05:00.436
their tools don't replace a human review.

117
00:05:00.886 --> 00:05:03.521
The house picture breaks in three places.

118
00:05:03.521 --> 00:05:06.830
An inspector can walk every room in a house.

119
00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:09.257
Nobody can walk every path through a program,

120
00:05:09.257 --> 00:05:12.229
so inspected never means proven safe.

121
00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:13.308
In software,

122
00:05:13.308 --> 00:05:15.990
the crew can write its own inspection report,

123
00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:19.055
and an AI inspector is another member of the crew,

124
00:05:19.055 --> 00:05:21.900
not an independent licensed authority.

125
00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.245
And burglars here try every door,

126
00:05:24.245 --> 00:05:27.078
of every house, at once, automatically.

127
00:05:27.078 --> 00:05:29.239
Now with AI too.

128
00:05:29.689 --> 00:05:33.369
Houses sit on a street, and a street has a style.

129
00:05:33.369 --> 00:05:35.807
Teams agree on naming, file layout,

130
00:05:35.807 --> 00:05:38.002
and how errors are handled.

131
00:05:38.002 --> 00:05:40.939
AI-written code can drift from those norms,

132
00:05:40.939 --> 00:05:43.191
and that makes life harder for the people

133
00:05:43.191 --> 00:05:45.443
who still write code by hand.

134
00:05:45.443 --> 00:05:47.614
It also tends to write a fresh copy

135
00:05:47.614 --> 00:05:50.354
instead of reusing the team's helper:

136
00:05:50.354 --> 00:05:53.652
one large analysis found more copy-pasted code

137
00:05:53.652 --> 00:05:56.833
and less reuse since AI took over.

138
00:05:56.833 --> 00:05:59.909
On a project with a set library of modules,

139
00:05:59.909 --> 00:06:03.218
AI may import different ones, and the hand-coders

140
00:06:03.218 --> 00:06:05.923
end up maintaining parts they never chose.

141
00:06:05.923 --> 00:06:08.048
So be mindful in mixed teams.

142
00:06:08.048 --> 00:06:11.020
Many open-source projects now ask you to disclose

143
00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.888
AI use and confirm a human read it.

144
00:06:13.888 --> 00:06:16.303
Curl even ended its bug bounty

145
00:06:16.303 --> 00:06:18.568
over AI junk reports.

146
00:06:19.019 --> 00:06:20.575
So the norms now have to be

147
00:06:20.575 --> 00:06:22.457
written down, for the crew.

148
00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:27.065
Claude Code reads a file called Claude .md.

149
00:06:27.065 --> 00:06:31.221
Many other tools read Agents .md, an open version

150
00:06:31.221 --> 00:06:34.054
now looked after by the Linux Foundation.

151
00:06:34.054 --> 00:06:36.294
One line in it might say:

152
00:06:36.294 --> 00:06:39.069
use only libraries already in this project.

153
00:06:39.069 --> 00:06:41.298
But it's a note, not a lock.

154
00:06:41.298 --> 00:06:43.133
Long files get skimmed;

155
00:06:43.133 --> 00:06:46.221
only checks and reviews actually enforce it.

156
00:06:46.221 --> 00:06:49.100
So someone who knows the team's norms has to write

157
00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:52.016
that file, and notice when it's ignored.

158
00:06:52.466 --> 00:06:54.846
Every house has suppliers.

159
00:06:54.846 --> 00:06:57.389
In code, they're dependencies:

160
00:06:57.389 --> 00:07:00.361
other people's libraries your program pulls in.

161
00:07:00.361 --> 00:07:03.147
Even when everyone on the team codes with AI,

162
00:07:03.147 --> 00:07:05.295
someone who knows the libraries should decide

163
00:07:05.295 --> 00:07:07.362
which ones the program uses.

164
00:07:07.362 --> 00:07:09.777
Otherwise AI keeps adding more,

165
00:07:09.777 --> 00:07:11.855
a new package for every want.

166
00:07:11.855 --> 00:07:13.678
Each one is a stranger's code,

167
00:07:13.678 --> 00:07:15.431
running with your keys.

168
00:07:15.431 --> 00:07:17.788
One study this year found agents' package

169
00:07:17.788 --> 00:07:20.691
choices made security worse on balance,

170
00:07:20.691 --> 00:07:22.664
where people's made it better.

171
00:07:22.664 --> 00:07:25.834
AI still sometimes names packages

172
00:07:25.834 --> 00:07:27.808
that don't exist, and attackers

173
00:07:27.808 --> 00:07:30.606
register the names it tends to invent.

174
00:07:30.606 --> 00:07:32.161
Self-spreading worms have hit

175
00:07:32.161 --> 00:07:34.298
the JavaScript package registry again

176
00:07:34.298 --> 00:07:36.724
and again since last September;

177
00:07:36.724 --> 00:07:39.221
this August's also hid files in a hijacked

178
00:07:39.221 --> 00:07:41.995
project's own code, set to run the moment

179
00:07:41.995 --> 00:07:44.886
someone opened it in an AI editor.

180
00:07:44.886 --> 00:07:46.407
You'll meet it as five new lines

181
00:07:46.407 --> 00:07:49.381
in your package list that nobody asked for.

182
00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.048
Then comes move-in day.

183
00:07:52.048 --> 00:07:54.671
Deploying is handing the keys to real tenants.

184
00:07:54.671 --> 00:07:56.726
You ask: is it ready to deploy?

185
00:07:56.726 --> 00:07:58.387
It's the same trap.

186
00:07:58.387 --> 00:08:00.627
The agent runs the checks it knows about,

187
00:08:00.627 --> 00:08:02.392
and reports success.

188
00:08:02.392 --> 00:08:05.480
It can't vouch for what nobody specified.

189
00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:07.338
Who may see which data.

190
00:08:07.338 --> 00:08:09.695
What it costs under real load.

191
00:08:09.695 --> 00:08:11.866
How to roll back when something breaks.

192
00:08:11.866 --> 00:08:13.909
So before you ship, have someone

193
00:08:13.909 --> 00:08:15.883
who can code verify it.

194
00:08:15.883 --> 00:08:18.541
Platforms now add pre-launch scanners,

195
00:08:18.541 --> 00:08:20.945
and that helps, but the leaks you just heard

196
00:08:20.945 --> 00:08:23.384
about happened in those same months.

197
00:08:23.835 --> 00:08:25.646
Where's the line today?

198
00:08:25.646 --> 00:08:27.039
AI is strongest

199
00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:29.675
where there's a machine-readable pass or fail,

200
00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:32.066
as long as the check itself is sound.

201
00:08:32.066 --> 00:08:34.876
It builds. The tests it was given pass.

202
00:08:34.876 --> 00:08:37.651
The linter and type checker come back clean.

203
00:08:37.651 --> 00:08:39.822
A scanner flags a known pattern.

204
00:08:39.822 --> 00:08:42.399
The page loads. It's weak on the rest.

205
00:08:42.399 --> 00:08:44.570
Whether the tests test the right thing.

206
00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:46.068
Whether it's what you meant.

207
00:08:46.068 --> 00:08:48.193
Who should be allowed to see what.

208
00:08:48.193 --> 00:08:50.619
And threats newer than the model.

209
00:08:50.619 --> 00:08:52.686
Checkers exist now.

210
00:08:52.686 --> 00:08:55.171
Claude Code has a security review command

211
00:08:55.171 --> 00:08:56.854
and a security plugin.

212
00:08:56.854 --> 00:09:00.128
Anthropic, OpenAI, Google and GitHub all

213
00:09:00.128 --> 00:09:03.321
have security agents in preview or beta,

214
00:09:03.321 --> 00:09:06.037
and review agents can look at every change.

215
00:09:06.037 --> 00:09:08.522
As of October 2026,

216
00:09:08.522 --> 00:09:10.577
every one of them is meant to sit beside

217
00:09:10.577 --> 00:09:13.411
a human reviewer, not replace one.

218
00:09:13.861 --> 00:09:15.707
Which brings us to the rule.

219
00:09:15.707 --> 00:09:18.087
You can only trust AI on what AI,

220
00:09:18.087 --> 00:09:20.177
or you, can verify.

221
00:09:20.177 --> 00:09:25.158
An old rule for trial lawyers, from 1975, says:

222
00:09:25.158 --> 00:09:28.432
don't ask a question you don't know the answer to.

223
00:09:28.432 --> 00:09:30.452
Adapted for AI:

224
00:09:30.452 --> 00:09:32.855
don't rely on an answer you can't check.

225
00:09:32.855 --> 00:09:35.351
Here's the whole lesson in one picture.

226
00:09:35.351 --> 00:09:37.603
Crashes, builds, tests:

227
00:09:37.603 --> 00:09:39.658
let the agent run those checks.

228
00:09:39.658 --> 00:09:43.722
Security, team fit, dependencies, and deploys:

229
00:09:43.722 --> 00:09:47.555
check them yourself, or get someone who can.

230
00:09:48.005 --> 00:09:49.758
And the line keeps moving outward,

231
00:09:49.758 --> 00:09:50.838
every few months.

232
00:09:50.838 --> 00:09:53.415
Separate verifier agents, so the one doing

233
00:09:53.415 --> 00:09:55.424
the work isn't the one grading it.

234
00:09:55.424 --> 00:09:57.281
Security agents that try to confirm

235
00:09:57.281 --> 00:09:59.138
a bug before they report it.

236
00:09:59.138 --> 00:10:02.981
The checks AI can run on itself get better fast,

237
00:10:02.981 --> 00:10:04.676
though the code it writes first time

238
00:10:04.676 --> 00:10:06.720
hasn't got much safer.

239
00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.868
Attackers get the same tools:

240
00:10:08.868 --> 00:10:12.165
AI is finding old bugs humans missed.

241
00:10:12.165 --> 00:10:14.533
Anthropic's own engineers expect Claude's

242
00:10:14.533 --> 00:10:16.727
code to beat theirs within a year.

243
00:10:16.727 --> 00:10:18.051
That's a forecast.

244
00:10:18.051 --> 00:10:20.001
Will the human yes go away?

245
00:10:20.001 --> 00:10:21.244
Nobody knows.

246
00:10:21.244 --> 00:10:23.556
Today, every tool keeps it.

247
00:10:24.006 --> 00:10:25.817
Safe to ignore for now:

248
00:10:25.817 --> 00:10:28.418
which scanner is best, the security jargon,

249
00:10:28.418 --> 00:10:30.450
and the exact percentages.

250
00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:32.609
Keep this. One.

251
00:10:32.609 --> 00:10:34.525
The agent fixes the crashes;

252
00:10:34.525 --> 00:10:36.638
you own what it can't see.

253
00:10:36.638 --> 00:10:39.343
Security, team fit, dependencies

254
00:10:39.343 --> 00:10:43.617
and ready to ship are judgment calls. Two.

255
00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:48.016
Yes, it's secure, is just another answer.

256
00:10:48.016 --> 00:10:50.814
If you can't check it, learn enough to check it,

257
00:10:50.814 --> 00:10:54.564
or get someone who can. Three.

258
00:10:54.564 --> 00:10:56.178
Someone has to own the rules

259
00:10:56.178 --> 00:10:57.791
and the supplier list.

260
00:10:57.791 --> 00:11:00.183
Write the team's norms down for the agent,

261
00:11:00.183 --> 00:11:01.530
and let a person who knows

262
00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.387
the libraries approve every new dependency.
